From 69ef1c6b22b87f5ab448bcc027918c75ee7e23a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mala <mala@macker.lol>
Date: Tue, 14 Jul 2026 00:31:06 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] soc: espressif: esp32s3: re-init esp_flash default chip after
 PSRAM init

On the ESP32-S3 the MSPI core clock is shared between flash and PSRAM
(SOC_MEMSPI_CORE_CLK_SHARED_WITH_PSRAM). PSRAM timing tuning (required
e.g. for octal PSRAM at 80 MHz) switches that core clock, but
esp_flash_config() has already initialized the esp_flash default chip
at that point, capturing the pre-tuning source frequency in the SPI1
clock configuration that every esp_flash transaction re-programs into
the controller.

After tuning raises the core clock from 80 to 160 MHz, the stale
divider of 1 clocks all SPI1 command transactions at 160 MHz. The
flash then returns garbage on status reads, and every erase/program
fails the WEL verification in spi_flash_chip_generic_set_write_protect
with ESP_ERR_NOT_FOUND (261) - the flash driver (and with it flash_map,
NVS, settings, ...) is unusable, while XIP through the tuned SPI0/cache
path keeps working.

ESP-IDF avoids this by initializing the default chip after PSRAM
timing tuning. Mirror that by re-running esp_flash_init_default_chip()
after esp_init_psram(): it recomputes the clock configuration from the
live (post-tuning) core clock.

---
 soc/espressif/esp32s3/soc.c | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/soc/espressif/esp32s3/soc.c b/soc/espressif/esp32s3/soc.c
index be973bee2b..1d4df15e85 100644
--- a/soc/espressif/esp32s3/soc.c
+++ b/soc/espressif/esp32s3/soc.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <esp_rom_serial_output.h>
 #include <soc_init.h>
 #include <flash_init.h>
+#include <esp_flash_internal.h>
 #include <esp_private/cache_utils.h>
 #include <esp_private/system_internal.h>
 #include <esp_timer.h>
@@ -61,6 +62,20 @@ void IRAM_ATTR __esp_platform_app_start(void)
 #if CONFIG_ESP_SPIRAM
 	esp_init_psram();
 
+	/* PSRAM timing tuning may have switched the MSPI core clock, which is
+	 * shared between flash and PSRAM. The esp_flash default chip captured
+	 * the pre-tuning source frequency in its SPI1 clock configuration, so
+	 * every esp_flash transaction would now overclock the bus (e.g. core
+	 * clock 80 -> 160 MHz with a stale divider of 1 drives the flash at
+	 * 160 MHz: status reads return garbage and every erase/program fails
+	 * in the WEL check with ESP_ERR_NOT_FOUND). ESP-IDF initializes the
+	 * default chip after PSRAM for this reason; re-run the init here to
+	 * recompute the clock configuration from the final core clock.
+	 */
+	if (esp_flash_init_default_chip() != ESP_OK) {
+		printk("Failed to re-init flash chip after PSRAM init\n");
+	}
+
 	int err = esp_psram_smh_init();
 
 	if (err) {
-- 
2.53.0

